              ":tutorial/content": "Suppose we are given the following matrix:\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nWe denote the entry that lies at the intersection of the $\\color{blue}3$rd row and $\\color{red}2$nd column using the notation $a_{{\\color{blue}3}\\color{red}2}.$\n\nThe first subscript represents the row and the second subscript represents the column. So $a_{{\\color{blue}3}\\color{red}2}=8,$ as shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & \\boxed{8} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can denote every entry of a matrix in this way.\nFor instance, $a_{{\\color{blue}4}\\color{red}1}$ denotes the entry that lies at the intersection of the $\\color{blue}4$th row and $\\color{red}1$st column.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & {a}_{13} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & {a}_{23} \\\\ {a}_{31} & {a}_{32} & {a}_{33} \\\\ {a}_{41} & {a}_{42} & {a}_{43}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
              ":tutorial/id": "34f3a63d-a273-50fb-b26a-ae172c7e93e9",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 2969,
              ":tutorial/title": "Introduction"
            },
            ":step/id": "71c4da2c-9ba7-5b55-a1fc-62db722468f0",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 462030,
            ":step/next": {
              ":db/id": 17592186101855
            }
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186101855,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186100988,
              ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
                ":db/id": 17592186100683,
                ":question/id": "622baf41-fe35-55ee-bfc6-f1e6a2cb089a",
                ":question/math-academy-id": "e-4371",
                ":question/problem": "Find the entry $a_{23}$ of the matrix ${A},$ where \n\n$$\n{A}\n=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
                ":question/worked-solution": "The entry $a_{23}$ lies at the intersection of the $2$nd row and $3$rd column. Therefore, $a_{23}={\\color{blue}{-3}}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"
              },
              ":knowledge-point/id": "8d783281-8364-5597-a804-a4ea0d76bec8",
              ":knowledge-point/key-prerequisites": [
                {
                  ":db/id": 17592186048896
                }
              ],
              ":knowledge-point/title": "Finding an Entry of a Matrix"
            },
            ":step/id": "557b3c46-5feb-5a02-969f-d023d0d4b18f",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 462031,
            ":step/next": {
              ":db/id": 17592186101856
            }
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186101856,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186100989,
              ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
                ":db/id": 17592186100684,
                ":question/id": "fe56a988-49e9-5f33-b0a5-f43d33984c1d",
                ":question/math-academy-id": "e-4372",
                ":question/problem": "Find the entry $b_{22}$ of the matrix ${B},$ where \n\n$$\n{B}\n=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
                ":question/worked-solution": "The entry $b_{22}$ lies at the intersection of the $2$nd row and $2$nd column. Therefore, $b_{22}={\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"
              },
              ":knowledge-point/id": "ecd07ea0-767d-55f0-a61c-da49dd320f89",
              ":knowledge-point/title": "Finding an Entry of a Square Matrix"
            },
            ":step/id": "f3dc9f2c-54de-514f-806c-13cfc1cafb33",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 462034,
            ":step/next": {
              ":db/id": 17592186101857
            }
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186101857,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186101953,
              ":tutorial/content": "In general, we denote the entry of a matrix $A$ that lies at the intersection of the $\\color{blue}i$th row and $\\color{red}j$th column by $a_{{\\color{blue}i}\\color{red}j}.$\n\nIf $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix, we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & \\cdots & {a}_{1n} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & \\cdots & {a}_{2n} \\\\ \\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots \\\\ {a}_{m1} & {a}_{m2} & \\cdots & {a}_{mn}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere each entry $a_{ij}$ is defined for $i = 1,2,\\ldots,m$ and $j =1,2,\\ldots,n.$",
              ":tutorial/id": "775ac64e-8b63-5c51-824d-1d99d17a2a0a",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 2970,
              ":tutorial/title": "General Form of a Matrix"
            },
            ":step/id": "bd3801a8-679e-5a33-b62a-575a32786880",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 462037,
            ":step/next": {
              ":db/id": 17592186101858
            }
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186101858,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186100990,
              ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
                ":db/id": 17592186100685,
                ":question/id": "432a6a1a-ba9d-5aa0-b758-d4d32e9992f3",
                ":question/math-academy-id": "e-4373",
                ":question/problem": "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $C$ given below. Describe it in terms of $c_{ij}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & \\boxed{5} \\\\ 34 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
                ":question/worked-solution": "The entry lies at the intersection of the $1$st row and $3$rd column. Therefore, we have $c_{13}={\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{5}}.$"
              },
              ":knowledge-point/id": "1cc70bda-40cf-5860-9a47-25f290c71041",
              ":knowledge-point/title": "Describing an Entry of a Matrix in Index Notation"
            },
            ":step/id": "456e8474-5f06-554c-9b60-c29b26039234",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 462038
